  abstract = {In clinical diagnosis, it is crucial and difficult to make a distinction among mental illnesses. As voice is one way of expression, it has been proved that it is a good indicator to separate mental illnesses from healthy people. Though many studies have used acoustic features to identify depression, few of them explored how to identify comorbidities from depressed people. We utilized a secondary audio data to detecting comorbid Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) and Dysthymia from pure depression, respectively. Two purposes of this study are: (1) to investigate the effects of acoustic features on identifying comorbidities from pure depression and (2) to compare the predictive performances between the two comorbidities under the state of natural experiment. Before extracted acoustic features from audios, we preprocessed the audio files at first. The procedure of voice preprocessing experienced three steps: matching and exclusion, screening and cutting and de-noising. After that, we extracted acoustic features from the preprocessed audios and utilized these features to identify comorbidities. Our results shown the best accuracy of identifying GAD and dysthymia are 70% and 79% respectively. By using dimension reduction, for GAD and dysthymia, the predictive powers grew 5% and 4.1% respectively. The results indicated that the depressed people with comorbidities can be effectively identified from pure depressed people via acoustic features. In addition, it is supported that the mo},
}

